.TH BTHID 1
.SH NAME
bthid \- a Bluetooth LE mouse into the pointer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bthid
[
.B -v
]
.I addr
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bthid
dials
.BI bt! addr !hid
-- so
.IR bt9p (4)
must be serving
.B /net/bt
-- and turns each report the device notifies into pointer movement,
writing
.BI "d " "dx dy buttons"
to
.BR /dev/pointer ,
as the USB mouse driver does. The boot mouse report is three bytes:
buttons (bit 0 left, 1 right, 2 middle), then dx and dy as signed
bytes.
.PP
A mouse sleeps and drops its link; when it moves it advertises
again, and
.I bthid
dials again, for as long as it runs. The first dial pairs if the
device is not known, which
.IR bt9p (4)
does only with
.B pairable
on.
.PP
Boot keyboard reports (eight bytes) are recognised and counted, not
typed: a keyboard wants the decoder in
.BR os/init/kbdusb.b ,
and there has been no keyboard to try it on.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
; cat /net/bt/lescan
c8:f3:e8:06:55:8b random -71 Microsoft Ocean Plastic Mouse
; echo pairable on > /net/bt/ctl
; bthid -v c8:f3:e8:06:55:8b &
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bthid.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bt9p (4),
.B docs/BLUETOOTH.md
